    <HwNote kind="grounded" label="The .dtb exists; getting U-Boot to load it was a separate problem — now solved">
      <p>
        On the first hardware boot U-Boot found our device tree on the card
        &mdash; <code>ext4ls</code> listed <code>force.dtb</code> next to the kernel
        and the OpenSBI payload &mdash; and then refused to boot, because its
        <code>k230_set_dtb</code> command does not read a device tree. It reads a
        text file named <code>force_dtb</code> whose <em>contents</em> name one.
        Two filenames a single character apart, with completely different jobs.
      </p>
      <p>
        With that sorted, U-Boot loads this tree and hands it to <code>bootm</code>,
        and on 2026-09-20 Linux booted from it. One caveat that bit afterwards: the
        <code>/chosen/bootargs</code> property in this tree is <em>not</em> what the
        kernel ends up reading, because U-Boot overwrites it. The kernel command
        line on this board is a property of the U-Boot environment.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="census" title="What the SoC file declares, by status">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Fifty-six hand-written nodes: forty-seven at the top level plus the nine
      children of <code>sysctl</code> and <code>security</code>. The interesting
      split is by <code>status</code> &mdash; nineteen sit disabled waiting for a
      board to claim them, twenty-five are switched on unconditionally, and twelve
      carry no <code>status</code> at all, which in device-tree semantics means
      enabled.
    </p>

    <p class="hw-p">
      After a board file has had its say the flattened tree is
      <strong>234 nodes: 184 okay, 11 still disabled</strong>. Those eleven are
      <code>uart1</code>, <code>uart2</code>, <code>uart4</code>,
      <code>i2c0</code>&ndash;<code>i2c2</code>, all three SPI controllers and
      <code>mipi1</code>/<code>mipi2</code>: everything nobody claimed. Anything
      this board needs from that list is a node we would have to enable ourselves,
      and the charger, the fuel gauge and the LoRa radio are all in it.
    </p>

    <ul class="hw-key">
      <li><i class="k-soc"></i>status = "okay" in k230.dtsi</li>
      <li><i class="st-implicit" style="width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%"></i>no status property — enabled by default</li>
      <li><i class="k-open"></i>status = "disabled" — a board must opt in</li>
      <li><i class="k-ref"></i>opted in by the board file</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="hw-band">
      <header>
        <h3>Core — always live</h3>
        <span class="tag">no board involvement possible</span>
        <span class="cnt" style="color:var(--soc)">12 nodes</span>
      </header>
      <div class="hw-tiles">
        <div class="hw-tile"><i class="st-implicit"></i><div><b>cpu@0</b><span>thead,c908 · sv39 · the only CPU in the tree</span></div></div>
        <div class="hw-tile"><i class="st-implicit"></i><div><b>l2-cache</b><span>256 KiB unified</span></div></div>
        <div class="hw-tile"><i class="st-implicit"></i><div><b>plic</b><span>interrupt-controller@f00000000<br />canaan,k230-plic / thead,c900-plic</span></div></div>
        <div class="hw-tile"><i class="st-implicit"></i><div><b>clint</b><span>timer@f04000000</span></div></div>
        <div class="hw-tile"><i class="st-implicit"></i><div><b>gpio0_ports</b><span>gpio@9140b000 · pins 0–31<br />gpio- and interrupt-controller</span></div></div>
        <div class="hw-tile"><i class="st-implicit"></i><div><b>gpio1_ports</b><span>gpio@9140c000 · pins 32–63</span></div></div>
        <div class="hw-tile"><i class="st-implicit"></i><div><b>watchdog1</b><span>wdt1@91106800 · snps,dw-wdt</span></div></div>
