    <HwNote label="Three things in that census that will bite">
      <p>
        <strong>The display chain is already on.</strong> <code>display</code>,
        <code>vo</code> and <code>dsi</code> are <code>okay</code> before any board
        file runs, and <code>vo</code> is already wired to <code>dsi</code> port 0 by
        an endpoint pair. A board never enables the display; it only hangs a panel off
        <code>&amp;dsi</code> port 1. That is why a panel fragment contains no
        <code>status</code> at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The SD and USB controllers sit outside the clock tree.</strong> There
        is a real clock provider &mdash; 150 nodes of PLLs, dividers and gates hanging
        off <code>sysctl_clock</code>, which the I²C, SPI, audio and display blocks all
        draw from. But both MMC controllers take
        <code>clocks = &lt;&amp;dummy_sd&gt;</code>, a <code>fixed-clock</code>
        asserting a flat 100 MHz, and the USB controllers take
        <code>clk_dummy</code> at 0 Hz. For those four the rate in the tree is an
        assertion, not a control &mdash; which is presumably why
        <code>mshc_ctrl_r</code> and the delay-line trims exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong><code>compatible = "canaan, k230-sysctl-power"</code> has a space in
        it.</strong> That looks like a typo, and it is, but
        <code>drivers/soc/canaan/k230-power-domains.c</code> carries exactly the same
        string in its match table. Correcting it would break the match.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="mechanism" title="How an override actually resolves">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      A board file does not copy a node. It writes <code>&amp;label &#123; … &#125;</code>,
      which the compiler resolves to whichever node carries that label anywhere in
      the included tree, then merges the properties in: later assignment wins,
      everything unmentioned survives.
    </p>

    <HwFigure
      caption="<strong>Rule 2 is the one that surprises people.</strong> The board file re-points <code>&amp;mipi0</code> by re-assigning <code>reg</code>, <code>interrupts</code> and <code>resets</code> &mdash; a node that was already <code>okay</code> in the SoC file quietly moves to a different address and a different interrupt. Nothing flags it, and our board file inherits that edit unchanged because the reference does it."
    >
      <svg
        viewBox="0 0 920 400"
        role="img"
        aria-label="The SoC node for uart0 with status disabled, plus a board fragment ampersand-uart0 setting status okay, merge into one effective node in which only status has changed."
      >
        <defs>
          <marker id="ov-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="var(--ink-2)"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <text x="8" y="18" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="1.1" fill="var(--soc)" font-family="var(--sans)">DECLARED — k230.dtsi</text>
        <rect x="8" y="28" width="292" height="174" rx="3" fill="var(--soc-f)" stroke="var(--soc)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect>
        <text x="22" y="50" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--mono)">uart0: serial@91400000 {"{"}</text>
        <text x="34" y="68" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--mono)">compatible = "snps,dw-apb-uart";</text>
        <text x="34" y="86" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--mono)">reg = &lt;0x0 0x91400000 …&gt;;</text>
        <text x="34" y="104" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--mono)">interrupts = &lt;16 LEVEL_HIGH&gt;;</text>
