  <HwSection kind="taxonomy" title="Seven things a board file does">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Read against the two reference boards and our own, every edit falls into one
      of these seven. There is no eighth kind, which is a useful thing to know
      before writing a board file.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-recipes">
      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">Kind 1 — flip a switch</p>
          <h3>Enable a controller, change nothing else</h3>
          <p>
            The commonest edit and the least interesting. <code>uart0</code>,
            <code>uart3</code>, <code>usb0</code>, <code>usb1</code> and
            <code>i2c4</code> get exactly this on our board.
          </p>
        </div>
        <pre class="hw-code" set:html={kind1} />
      </div>

      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">Kind 2 — flip and tune</p>
          <h3>Enable, then add board-electrical properties</h3>
          <p>
            Signal-integrity and voltage facts only the board knows.
            <code>rx_delay_line</code> and <code>tx_delay_line</code> are
            Canaan-specific delay-chain trims; <code>no-1-8-v</code> says the level
            shifters cannot do 1.8&nbsp;V signalling.
          </p>
        </div>
        <pre class="hw-code" set:html={kind2} />
      </div>

      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">Kind 3 — attach a device</p>
          <h3>Enable a bus and declare what hangs off it</h3>
          <p>
            The child node&rsquo;s unit address is the I²C address. GPIOs are
            referenced through the bank controller&rsquo;s label and a per-bank index.
            This is the shape of our GT9895 node, shown as we actually wrote it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <pre class="hw-code" set:html={kind3} />
      </div>

      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">Kind 4 — re-point</p>
          <h3>Rewrite reg, interrupts and resets on a live node</h3>
          <p>
            The board tells <code>mipi0</code> it is a CSI-2 receiver and hands it a
            different register window and interrupt. Note there is no
            <code>status</code> here &mdash; the node was already <code>okay</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            An unconditional rewrite of a hardware address from a board file is a
            strong smell. It is also what upstream does, and we carry it over rather
            than diverge on something we have not tested.
          </p>
        </div>
        <pre class="hw-code" set:html={kind4} />
      </div>

      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">Kind 5 — graft an endpoint</p>
          <h3>Extend an existing port graph</h3>
          <p>
            The SoC file leaves <code>&amp;dsi</code> with only <code>port@0</code>
            filled. A board adds <code>port@1</code> and the endpoint naming its
            downstream sink &mdash; a bridge chip on one board, a panel on ours.
            Endpoints are declared in pairs and both halves must name each other or
            the bridge chain never forms.
          </p>
        </div>
        <pre class="hw-code" set:html={kind5} />
      </div>

      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">Kind 6 — new root nodes</p>
          <h3>Facts that have no SoC counterpart</h3>
          <p>
            How much DRAM, which UART is the console, what the board is called, the
            machine-level sound card. These are written into <code>/</code> directly
            because there is nothing upstream to reference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>aliases</code> matters more than it looks: it fixes the
            <code>ttySn</code> and <code>mmcblkn</code> numbering the rest of the
            system will see.
          </p>
        </div>
        <pre class="hw-code" set:html={kind6} />
      </div>

      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">Kind 7 — include a fragment</p>
          <h3>Pull in a whole subsystem description</h3>
          <p>
            One <code>#include</code> is the entire difference between the plain
            CanMV v3 board and the LCD variant. The fragment is not a node &mdash; it
            is a bundle of <code>&amp;</code>-overrides that happens to live in its own
            file, which is why it can be reused across boards.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is the mechanism our port uses: write the panel fragment, include it
            instead, and attach the pins by label afterwards.
          </p>
        </div>
        <pre class="hw-code" set:html={kind7} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the real thing" title="The board file we actually wrote">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      An earlier version of this note carried a sketch of what a T-Display board
      file might look like, with a Wi-Fi power sequence and a
      <code>bus-width</code> override in it, and a footnote saying it had never been
      compiled. The file below is the one in the repository. It compiles against the
      real <code>k230.dtsi</code>, every node was verified present in the output
      rather than assumed, and the two guessed-at Wi-Fi edits are not in it.
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={boardFile} />

    <HwNote kind="ours" label="What the sketch proposed and the real file does not do">
      <p>
        The sketch added <code>mmc-pwrseq-simple</code> with
        <code>reset-gpios = &lt;&amp;gpio1_ports 13&gt;</code>, plus
        <code>bus-width = &lt;4&gt;</code>, <code>non-removable</code> and
        <code>cap-sdio-irq</code> on <code>&amp;mmc_sd0</code>. None of that is in the
        committed file. IO45 drives an LDO enable, not a card reset, and the polarity
        was never confirmed against the RT9080 footprint; a fixed regulator plus
        <code>vmmc-supply</code> may be the more honest model. Leaving the gap visible
        beats encoding a guess in a file that compiles.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="What compiling does and does not prove">
      <p>
        It proves the description is well-formed and that every label it reaches for
        exists. It says nothing about whether the panel lights, whether the timings are
        right, or whether the reset polarity is correct. Linux now boots from this
        tree &mdash; to a root shell, on 2026-09-20 &mdash; and the panel has still
        never shown anything.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two syntax mistakes cost time and are worth recording, because both produce
        misleading errors. A DTS bytestring takes <em>bare</em> hex pairs, so
        <code>[0x39 0x00 …]</code> fails with a plain &ldquo;syntax error&rdquo;
        pointing at the opening bracket. And all properties must precede any subnode
        &mdash; a <code>panel-init-sequence</code> sitting after
        <code>display-timings</code> gets &ldquo;Properties must precede
        subnodes&rdquo;, naming the whole node rather than the property.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <div class="hw-sources">
    <p>
      Census taken by walking arch/riscv/boot/dts/canaan/k230.dtsi at
      ruyisdk/linux-xuantie-kernel 7d4e1f444f461dbe3833bd99a4640e7b6c2cd529. Byte
      counts and node counts are from running cpp and dtc over each board file at that
      commit and re-reading the output.
    </p>
    <p>
      Board evidence: the vendored U-Boot device tree k230_canmv_v3p0.dts, the RT-Smart
      k230_canmv_v3p0_defconfig, and the V1.0_NEW schematic.
    </p>
  </div>
</HardwareNote>
